Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Room Breathes Before We Do
- Story & Context: During the ensemble's first public test inside the cistern, Ilaria begins the opening drone before the sound system has been switched on. A technician walking along the rear wall notices that one limestone panel is vibrating strongly enough to dust the floor with pale powder. Malik records the tiny impacts and builds a footwork pattern from them in real time, but every attempt to add a conventional hi-hat makes the rhythm feel smaller. He deletes the hats and allows the wall itself to become the top percussion.

The piece develops as an argument between deliberate performance and discovered acoustics. Prepared cello enters with one bowed harmonic that keeps beating against the launeddas drone. The electronic kick clusters grow denser, yet the mineral clicks remain untouched and slightly unstable. Midway through the track, the audience is asked to shift half a meter backward because their bodies are damping the strongest reflection. That movement changes the sound enough to become the arrangement's turning point.

The final section cuts the sub-bass entirely. Ilaria continues the same circular breath while the stone ticks become more audible than the electronics. The song closes with the realization that the ensemble did not activate the room; the room had already been articulating its own rhythm, and the musicians merely learned where to stand.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Underground limestone chamber under narrow utility lighting, pale calcite dust falling from a vibrating wall panel, chalk position marks on a dark stone floor, three reed pipes visible in close profile, scattered cables and a small field recorder, hyper-real documentary texture, subdued mineral palette. Any vocal fragments should be sparse rehearsal language: "move back," "hold the air," "leave the wall alone," repeated rhythmically and processed into short footwork cuts.

Track 2: Five Heels Past the Water Mark
- Story & Context: Giada finds an old flood line running across the lower wall of the rehearsal chamber, exactly five heel-lengths above the current floor after a later renovation changed the room's level. She begins marking the distance with her shoe while Tomas records each heel impact through a contact microphone. The five-step pattern becomes a recurring rhythmic cell that refuses to fit neatly inside the 4/4 footwork grid.

Malik answers by writing a kick pattern that resets every forty beats rather than every four bars. The launeddas part uses a narrow three-note melodic range, repeating almost stubbornly while the beat cycles through different alignments beneath it. Giada's cello is prepared with a folded transit ticket under one string, producing a papery buzz that functions like a snare ghost note.

The track's narrative centers on the physical evidence of vanished water. No character explains the flood historically. Instead, the musicians measure it through body scale: heel, hand, breath, bow length. In the final passage, all programmed drums drop out and the four members walk the five-step measure together. Their shoes land slightly differently, revealing that the supposedly fixed unit was never exact.
